{
 "summary": "Found seven actionable defects in retry identity, refund handling, discount taxation, and reconciliation. All were reproduced against the supplied TypeScript implementation. No files were changed.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Distinct purchases collide because the key ignores requestId",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:42-45",
   "failure": "Two requests with different requestIds but the same customer, currency, subtotal, and day return the same entry. The second purchase never posts. Different items or promos can also collide because the key ignores them. Reproduced with requestIds a and b: both returned led_1.",
   "fix": "Use the customer and requestId as the purchase identity, rather than the subtotal and posting day."
  },
  {
   "title": "A retry on another day posts a second charge",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:43-45",
   "failure": "The key depends on the server's current day, not stable request identity. Replaying an unchanged request after the UTC day boundary bypasses the original entry. Reproduced at timestamps 0 and 86400000: the same request created led_1 and led_2.",
   "fix": "Remove the posting-day bucket from request identity. Retain the original result under a stable customer/requestId key."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refunds apply the processing fee a second time",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:88",
   "failure": "charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Applying applyProcessingFee again makes a full refund larger than the original payment. A charge of 11190 minor units produced a refund of -11515, over-refunding 325.",
   "fix": "Negate the original charge.grossCents directly. Do not gross up a stored gross amount."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refund retries append multiple compensating entries",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:92-100",
   "failure": "refund assigns a stable key but never looks it up. Ledger.append unconditionally pushes another entry and overwrites the key index at ledger.ts:10-13. Two calls for the same charge produced distinct refund entries led_3 and led_4.",
   "fix": "Look up refund:<charge.id> before posting and return the original refund when present."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refund conversion uses a new rate instead of reversing the original settlement",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:89-90",
   "failure": "A full reversal recalculates settlement using the latest rate. An EUR charge with gross 10 settled to 10 USD cents at rate 1. After updating the rate to 1.2, its refund had gross -10 but settled -12. Thus the original charge and its full reversal leave a settlement residual, independently of the processing-fee defect.",
   "fix": "Reverse the stored settledCents and retain the original conversion rate. If exchange gains or losses are required, represent them separately rather than changing the charge reversal."
  },
  {
   "title": "Fixed discounts leave tax calculated on the undiscounted subtotal",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "failure": "Percentage discounts reduce the taxable base, but fixed discounts do not. A fixed discount covering a 10000-cent subtotal leaves an 875-cent total before fees, while a 100% discount leaves zero. Equivalent discounts therefore charge different amounts.",
   "fix": "Calculate the discount first, then calculate tax on the discounted subtotal through one shared calculation."
  },
  {
   "title": "Reconciliation reports mismatches for equal integer-cent balances",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "reconcile.ts:17-19",
   "failure": "statementTotalDollars divides each entry before summing, then reconcile compares floating-point dollars with exact inequality. Real charges with subtotals 1 and 5 produced settled amounts 1 and 5. Reconciliation against 6 cents returned a discrepancy because their dollar sum was 0.060000000000000005.",
   "fix": "Sum and compare settledCents as integers. Convert the completed sum to dollars only for display or the discrepancy payload."
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Ran two read-only Bun probes importing the actual billing, ledger, rates, and reconciliation modules. Each reported finding has a concrete reproduced input and result. No test suite or project gate was supplied."
}

{
 "summary": "Found nine actionable defects: incorrect refund amounts, duplicate refunds, unstable purchase identity, inconsistent discount taxation, refund FX drift, two rounding errors, and false reconciliation alerts. All examples were reproduced against the supplied implementation with Bun. No files were modified.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Refunds apply the processing fee a second time",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:88",
   "failure": "The stored charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Calling applyProcessingFee again refunds more than the customer paid. A 10,000-minor-unit subtotal produces a charge of 11,190 and a refund of 11,515, even with an unchanged exchange rate.",
   "fix": "Negate the charge's stored grossCents directly. Do not recalculate its processing fee."
  },
  {
   "title": "Distinct purchases collide on the same idempotency key",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:44-45",
   "failure": "The key excludes requestId, line-item identity, and the promo. Two different purchases by the same customer with the same currency and subtotal on one day return the same ledger entry. The probe submitted requestIds purchase-1 and purchase-2, with a 50% promo on the second purchase; both returned led_1 and only one charge existed.",
   "fix": "Identify purchases with the existing requestId, scoped to the customer as appropriate, rather than their subtotal."
  },
  {
   "title": "A retry across midnight posts another charge",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:43-45",
   "failure": "The key includes the server's current day rather than stable request identity. Replaying an unchanged request at timestamps 86,399,999 and 86,400,000 created led_1 and led_2. A timeout followed by a retry across midnight therefore double-charges the purchase.",
   "fix": "Remove the posting-day bucket from purchase identity. Retain and look up the original requestId-based key across retries."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refund retries append additional reversals",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:92-100; ledger.ts:10-13",
   "failure": "refund assigns a deterministic refund key but never checks it. Ledger.append also does not enforce uniqueness; its Map.set merely replaces the index entry after appending another record. Two calls to refund for one charge produced led_2 and led_3 with the same refund key and two negative amounts.",
   "fix": "Return the existing entry for refund:<charge.id> before creating another refund, using the same replay semantics as charge."
  },
  {
   "title": "Fixed discounts calculate tax on the undiscounted subtotal",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "failure": "The fixed branch calculates tax before subtracting the discount, unlike the percent branch. For a 10,000 subtotal, a fixed 1,000 discount produces tax 875 and total 9,875; an equivalent 10% discount produces tax 788 and total 9,788. A fixed discount covering the entire subtotal still charges 875 in tax before processing fees.",
   "fix": "Calculate the discounted subtotal first for both promo kinds, then apply the common tax calculation."
  },
  {
   "title": "Refunds revalue the original settlement instead of reversing it",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:89-90",
   "failure": "The documented full reversal reads the current exchange rate instead of reversing the stored USD settlement. An isolated probe charged 10 EUR minor units at rate 1 and refunded the same 10 minor units at rate 1.2. The entries settled at +10 and -12 USD cents, leaving a -2-cent balance even though the processing-fee rounding was unchanged.",
   "fix": "For a full reversal, negate the original settledCents and preserve its fxRate. Any separately intended realized FX adjustment needs its own explicit accounting policy and entry."
  },
  {
   "title": "Percentage discount rounding loses a minor unit at exact halves",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:29",
   "failure": "Binary floating-point division happens before multiplication. computeTotal(25, {kind:'percent', value:58, code:'p'}) returns discountCents 14, although 25 × 58 / 100 is exactly 14.5 and the code's Math.round policy requires 15. This overcharges the customer by a minor unit.",
   "fix": "Calculate percentage discounts with exact decimal or integer-rational arithmetic, then round once in customer minor units."
  },
  {
   "title": "FX settlement rounds exact half-cents down",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:108-109",
   "failure": "The conversion uses binary floating-point major units before final rounding. Charging a subtotal of 89 EUR cents produces grossCents 100. At rate 1.005, the implementation stores settledCents 100, although the exact conversion is 100.5 USD cents and must round to 101 under its rounding policy.",
   "fix": "Represent the decimal exchange rate exactly and perform a scaled conversion with one final rounding operation into USD minor units."
  },
  {
   "title": "Reconciliation falsely flags equal cent balances",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "reconcile.ts:8,17-18",
   "failure": "The statement adds floating-point dollar amounts and reconciliation compares them with strict equality. Real charges settling to 10 and 20 cents produced computedDollars 0.30000000000000004. reconcile(entries, 30) returned a discrepancy and would spuriously escalate a correctly balanced statement.",
   "fix": "Sum and compare integer settledCents. Convert the final sum to dollars only for display and discrepancy output."
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Two successful Bun stdin probes exercised the actual BillingService, Ledger, RateTable, computeTotal, statementTotalDollars, and reconcile implementations. Integer-rational calculations independently confirmed the percentage and FX rounding expectations. No project-wide gate was run."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings:

1. P1: Settled payouts are omitted from nightly recovery. Location: registry.ts:23 (added registration); backfill.ts:8,16-18. The new kind is registered for live ingest but is absent from BACKFILLED_KINDS, so archived payout.settled events are silently skipped and outages cannot be healed. A Bun probe returned the payout ID in skipped. Add the kind to the backfill configuration and test valid and invalid archived payouts.

2. P2: The added registry test does not exercise production wiring. Location: payout.test.ts:6-9 (new-file line numbers). It builds a private registry containing the validator, so it still passes if PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS omits the validator, and it never calls either production consumer. Extend the existing registry.test.ts production-path tests to assert live acceptance and rejection and backfill handling for payout events.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: 8 confirmed defects affect payment identity, refunds, discounts, settlement rounding, and reconciliation. All reproductions used the supplied implementation.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "Use purchase identity instead of subtotal and posting day for idempotency",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:42-45",
   "failure": "The key ignores requestId. Two distinct purchases with the same customer, currency, subtotal, and day return the same entry, silently dropping the second purchase. Different promotions also collide. Conversely, replaying an identical request across midnight generates another charge. Both behaviors were reproduced.",
   "fix": "Key charges by the stable requestId, scoped by customer, without including the posting day or calculated subtotal."
  },
  {
   "title": "Return the original refund on replay",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:87-100; ledger.ts:10-13",
   "failure": "refund() assigns a stable idempotency key but never checks it. Ledger.append() accepts duplicate keys and overwrites only the lookup map. Calling refund() twice produced two separate negative entries for the same charge, allowing repeated full refunds.",
   "fix": "Look up refund:${charge.id} before calculating or appending another refund, and return the existing entry."
  },
  {
   "title": "Do not apply another processing fee when reversing a charge",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:88",
   "failure": "charge.grossCents already includes the processing fee. Applying applyProcessingFee() again increases the refund above the customer's payment. A reproduced USD charge of 11,190 cents generated a refund of 11,515 cents.",
   "fix": "Reverse charge.grossCents directly without applying another fee."
  },
  {
   "title": "Reverse the booked settlement instead of converting the refund at a new rate",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:89-90",
   "failure": "A full reversal uses the current FX rate rather than the charge's recorded settlement. RateTable.update() makes rate changes reachable. An EUR charge with grossCents=1 settled to 1 USD cent at rate 1; its refund settled to -2 cents after the rate changed to 2. This leaves a ledger residual even when fee rounding adds nothing.",
   "fix": "Negate the original settledCents and retain its conversion rate for the reversal."
  },
  {
   "title": "Apply fixed discounts before calculating tax",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "billing.ts:34-36",
   "failure": "The fixed-discount branch taxes the original subtotal, unlike the percentage-discount branch. For a 10,000-cent subtotal, a fixed 5,000-cent discount produces a 5,875-cent total, while an equivalent 50% discount produces 5,438 cents. A fixed discount covering the entire subtotal still charges 875 cents in tax.",
   "fix": "Calculate tax on the subtotal remaining after the capped fixed discount, consistent with percentage discounts."
  },
  {
   "title": "Calculate percentage discounts without binary half-cent errors",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:29",
   "failure": "Dividing the percentage first introduces floating-point error before rounding. computeTotal(50, {kind:\"percent\", value:29, code:\"SAVE29\"}) returned a 14-cent discount instead of 15 cents. The resulting charge was 40 cents instead of 39 cents.",
   "fix": "Use exact scaled-integer or decimal percentage arithmetic, with one explicit rounding step to customer minor units."
  },
  {
   "title": "Avoid losing settlement cents during FX conversion",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "billing.ts:108-109",
   "failure": "Converting integer minor units into binary fractional major units can move exact half-cent results below the rounding boundary. A reproduced EUR charge with grossCents=29 and rate=0.5 settled to 14 USD cents instead of 15. A gross amount of 100 at rate 1.005 also settled to 100 instead of 101.",
   "fix": "Use decimal or scaled-integer FX arithmetic and round once to USD minor units, without the intermediate binary fractional major amount."
  },
  {
   "title": "Reconcile integer cents rather than floating-point dollar sums",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "reconcile.ts:8,17-19",
   "failure": "The function sums fractional dollars and compares them with exact equality. Two valid charges settling to 1 and 5 cents produced computedDollars=0.060000000000000005, so reconcile(entries, 6) returned a discrepancy despite matching balances. This triggers the documented finance escalation spuriously.",
   "fix": "Sum settledCents and compare integer cents directly. Convert the final total to dollars only for display or discrepancy reporting."
  }
 ],
 "verification": "Confirmed the reported behaviors with narrow Bun 1.4.4 probes through the supplied public functions and BillingService. No project files were changed."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings: P1: The plan relies on a nonexistent pin-ledger test, an unpublished web-tree-sitter version, and a runbook excluded from the extension package. It also mistakes an OpenAI caret range for an exact pin and describes OpenTUI alignment that already exists. The supplied snapshot omits implementation and extension build files, so several proposed checks cannot run here.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "title": "The pin ledger and its green baseline do not exist in this snapshot",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "plan.md:7,21,44-54,70,124",
   "failure": "The listed tests/unit/scripts directory contains no dependency-pins.test.ts, and searching the unit tree finds no dependency-pin ledger; bun test tests/unit/scripts/dependency-pins.test.ts exits 1 with no matching test files. There is nothing to extend or establish as an existing green baseline, and phase 1 verification cannot run as written.",
   "fix": "Create a pin-ledger test with the required existing-hold and lockstep assertions, correct the baseline and describe the new test as new coverage."
  },
  {
   "title": "The proposed web-tree-sitter version is unpublished and violates the current peer pin",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "plan.md:75-89,120-121,136-138",
   "failure": "package.json:85,90,107 records core 0.5.1 and web-tree-sitter 0.25.10, explaining the exact peer hold. A fresh npm registry query lists 0.25.0 through 0.25.10, and npm view web-tree-sitter@0.25.12 version returns E404. npm view @opentui/core@0.5.1 peerDependencies --json confirms an exact 0.25.10 peer. A fresh install cannot resolve the requested target; even if 0.25.12 were later published it would not satisfy that peer. Registry sources: https://registry.npmjs.org/web-tree-sitter and https://registry.npmjs.org/@opentui/core/0.5.1.",
   "fix": "Resolve the conflict in the requested target before implementation. Retain 0.25.10 unless an available, peer-compatible upgrade and its acceptance criteria are approved."
  },
  {
   "title": "OpenAI is a floating range, not an exact pin",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "plan.md:6,24,59-66,122",
   "failure": "package.json:102 declares openai as ^6.39.0, not 6.39.0. Keeping it unchanged cannot satisfy the plan's exact-pin success criterion, and the statement that its resolution cannot drift between installs is false without a lockfile qualification.",
   "fix": "Resolve whether OpenAI must be converted to 6.39.0 or remain out of scope, and make the task, tests, and success criterion consistent with that decision."
  },
  {
   "title": "The release runbook is excluded from packaged extensions",
   "severity": "P1",
   "location": "plan.md:97-116,123,141-143",
   "failure": "vscode/.vscodeignore:7 excludes docs/** and line 10 re-includes only docs/about-flywheel.md. Editing vscode/docs/releasing.md alone will not put it into the VSIX, so the inventory and installed-user acceptance criteria cannot pass.",
   "fix": "Include vscode/.vscodeignore in phase 3 and re-include docs/releasing.md while preserving the existing about-flywheel exception. Inspect the actual package inventory after packaging."
  },
  {
   "title": "The assumed OpenTUI baseline and native-asset staging proof are wrong",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "plan.md:6,8,44-54",
   "failure": "package.json:90-91 already pins both OpenTUI packages at 0.5.1, so there is no current core 0.2.15 manifest version to raise. tests/unit/scripts/binary-native-assets.test.ts:59-79 uses core 0.5.1 and a deliberately stale native-package fixture 0.2.15, then asserts the list returned by targetNativePackagesToInstall. It does not stage a package or prove that the build replaced one.",
   "fix": "Correct the baseline and scope phase 1 to enforcing the existing alignment. Treat the fixture as mismatch-selection coverage, and add real build/staging verification only if replacement is required."
  },
  {
   "title": "Lockstep equality alone does not enforce exact reproducible pins",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "plan.md:45,51-53,120,132-133",
   "failure": "The proposed rule only asserts that the two dependency strings match. Both entries could change together to ^0.5.1 and still satisfy equality while allowing floating versions, contrary to the exact-pin pattern in plan.md:15 and the 0.5.1 resolution goal at line 120. The current manifest happens to have exact values at package.json:90-91.",
   "fix": "Require both exact version syntax and equality, or assert the intended exact version, and include a regression scenario for matching ranges."
  },
  {
   "title": "The floating-range conversion has no actionable selection",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "plan.md:58-66",
   "failure": "The task refers to floating ranges 'the audit marks' but names neither the audit nor the dependencies to convert. package.json:92,98-100,102-104 contains several caret runtime ranges, while the same task explicitly exempts openai. The snapshot does not identify which remaining entries are intended targets, so different implementations can all claim compliance.",
   "fix": "List the exact packages and target versions or provide the authoritative audit, including which ranges deliberately remain floating."
  },
  {
   "title": "Phase 2 verification does not verify the changed dependency",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "plan.md:86-94,124",
   "failure": "The phase promises a fresh-install resolution and a correct manifest note but only runs opentui-native-package-patch.test.ts and binary-native-assets.test.ts. The former tests source-string rewrites at tests/unit/scripts/opentui-native-package-patch.test.ts:7-74; the latter tests fabricated package metadata and WASM filenames at tests/unit/scripts/binary-native-assets.test.ts:49-79. Neither checks installation, the web-tree-sitter version, the note, or the promised ledger after the phase.",
   "fix": "Add explicit isolated fresh-install and manifest-note checks, plus the ledger check, once a valid compatible target is decided."
  },
  {
   "title": "The snapshot cannot execute the proposed test and packaging commands",
   "severity": "P2",
   "location": "plan.md:70,93,115,125",
   "failure": "Both native-asset test files import omitted scripts at tests/unit/scripts/binary-native-assets.test.ts:6-13 and tests/unit/scripts/opentui-native-package-patch.test.ts:3; running them in this snapshot fails with module-not-found errors. The snapshot has no vscode/package.json, and running bun run package darwin-arm64 from vscode exits with Script not found. Root test, lint, and boundary commands also point to missing scripts in package.json:17,54,75. The full project is necessary to establish the requested green results.",
   "fix": "Run the plan against a complete project checkout and verify the named commands and packaging entry point there. Do not claim the partial snapshot demonstrates these gates."
  }
 ],
 "verification_notes": "Confirmed marked 17.0.1 and web-tree-sitter 0.25.10 at package.json:101,107; confirmed about-flywheel.md is the sole docs exception at vscode/.vscodeignore:7-10. Runtime code for the help surface and actual packaging scripts are absent from the snapshot, so their implementation claims remain unverified."
}

{
 "summary": "Findings:

1. P1: Payout events are excluded from recovery. Location: registry.ts:23 (added registration); backfill.ts:8,16-18. Registering the validator enables live ingest, but BACKFILLED_KINDS does not include payout.settled. The nightly job therefore skips both valid and malformed archived payouts before validation, so webhook outages cannot be healed for this kind. A probe of the patched code accepted a payout through ingest and returned its ID in backfill.skipped. Fix: add the kind to the backfill configuration and cover valid and malformed payout replay.

2. P2: New tests do not exercise production wiring. Location: payout.test.ts:6-9. The test constructs a separate registry directly from payoutSettledValidator, so it passes even if PRODUCTION_VALIDATORS omits the validator and never tests ingestEvent or runBackfill. All three added tests passed while the real backfill skipped the event. Fix: extend the existing production-consumer tests to assert live ingestion and archived replay of payout events, including invalid payloads.",
 "files_modified": []
}

{
 "summary": "The plan is not implementation-ready. It specifies an unpublished web-tree-sitter version, incorrectly excludes the floating OpenAI SDK pin, assumes a nonexistent green pin ledger, and promises a runbook that packaging explicitly excludes. The OpenTUI alignment is already complete. Snapshot omissions also prevent the proposed native-test, packaging, and final-gate verification.",
 "files_modified": [],
 "findings": [
  {
   "Title": "The proposed web-tree-sitter release does not exist and contradicts the exact peer hold",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-2/t1; plan.md:75-93,121,136-138",
   "Failure": "package.json:85 states that web-tree-sitter is held because @opentui/core requires that exact peer; package.json:90,107 pin core 0.5.1 and web-tree-sitter 0.25.10. A fresh npm registry probe returned only 0.25.0 through 0.25.10 for the 0.25 line. GET https://registry.npmjs.org/web-tree-sitter/0.25.12 returned HTTP 404 and 'version not found: 0.25.12'. The published metadata at https://registry.npmjs.org/@opentui/core/0.5.1 also returned peerDependencies.web-tree-sitter='0.25.10'. Thus 0.25.12 is not the final patch release, cannot satisfy the fresh-install criterion, and would not preserve the documented exact-peer relationship even if it existed.",
   "Fix": "Resolve the required bump against actual published versions and the core peer contract before scheduling implementation. Do not invent a replacement version or merely rewrite the hold note."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The runbook cannot reach installed users under the existing packaging filter",
   "Severity": "P1",
   "Location": "phase-3/t1; plan.md:3,18,98-116,123,141-143",
   "Failure": "vscode/.vscodeignore:7 excludes docs/**. Its only documentation exception is !docs/about-flywheel.md at vscode/.vscodeignore:8-10. The plan instead claims the whole docs directory ships and changes only vscode/docs/releasing.md. That leaves docs/releasing.md excluded, so the inventory and installed-runbook acceptance criteria cannot pass.",
   "Fix": "Include vscode/.vscodeignore in phase 3 and explicitly allow docs/releasing.md. Retain the existing exclusions and about-flywheel exception. Add an explicit packaged-file inventory assertion for the runbook rather than relying on package creation alone."
  },
  {
   "Title": "OpenAI is a floating range, not an untouched exact pin",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2; plan.md:6,24,59-66,122",
   "Failure": "package.json:102 declares openai as '^6.39.0', not '6.39.0'. A fresh Bun.semver.satisfies('6.40.0', '^6.39.0') probe returned true. Leaving this entry untouched cannot deliver the stated exact-6.39.0 success criterion and preserves the resolution drift the plan claims is impossible.",
   "Fix": "Correct the baseline and explicitly include the OpenAI range-to-pin decision. To meet the stated exact-6.39.0 outcome, change the manifest entry to '6.39.0' and cover its exactness in the ledger."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The claimed existing green pin ledger is absent",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "context.constraints; phase-1/t1; plan.md:3,7,21,39-54,70,124,130-133",
   "Failure": "The supplied tests/unit/scripts tree contains no dependency-pins.test.ts, and its contents contain no existing manifest-pin assertions to extend. Running the exact claimed baseline command, 'bun test tests/unit/scripts/dependency-pins.test.ts', exited 1 with 'filters did not match any test files'. The claimed green baseline is therefore not reproducible in this snapshot. Existing tests/unit/scripts/binary-native-assets.test.ts:59-79 cover a temporary native-package fixture, not manifest pin policy; tests/unit/scripts/manifest.test.ts:27 identifies a different owner, binary manifest generation.",
   "Fix": "Replace the false historical baseline with the observed result. Define creation of the ledger and its complete intended assertions, including deliberate holds and changed exact pins, rather than instructing an implementer to extend nonexistent coverage."
  },
  {
   "Title": "The floating-range conversion task does not identify its inputs or target versions",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-1/t2; plan.md:59-66,127",
   "Failure": "The task refers to ranges 'the audit marks for exactness', but the plan supplies neither that audit nor a conversion list. package.json:92,98-100,102-104 contains floating runtime entries for @solid-primitives/keyed, fuzzysort, highlight.js, js-yaml, openai, partial-json, and sharp. The task's scenarios specify only unchanged marked, web-tree-sitter, and openai entries. An implementer cannot determine which remaining ranges must change or which exact versions to select from this production handoff.",
   "Fix": "List each selected dependency with its current range and intended exact target. Explicitly identify any deliberate retained ranges. Add matching ledger coverage for the selected conversions."
  },
  {
   "Title": "Phase 2 verification does not prove its dependency-change claims",
   "Severity": "P2",
   "Location": "phase-2 verification; plan.md:86-94,124,138",
   "Failure": "tests/unit/scripts/opentui-native-package-patch.test.ts:8-73 tests transformations of synthetic OpenTUI source strings. tests/unit/scripts/binary-native-assets.test.ts:49-56 asserts grammar filenames, and :59-79 uses temporary core/sharp package manifests. Neither test reads the root web-tree-sitter pin, checks its note, resolves that package, or initializes its WASM parser. The phase's command also omits the ledger despite the success criterion requiring it after every phase. Passing these tests would not establish the stated fresh-install or hold-note outcomes.",
   "Fix": "After resolving the invalid version target, verify the actual manifest hold and note through the ledger, run a fresh dependency-resolution check, and use a parser/WASM check if the dependency changes. Do not describe unrelated OpenTUI tests as proof of the web-tree-sitter bump."
  },
  {
